You're running a pass over a page the user is writing, in the web UI of ws, which manages this multi-repo workspace (/ws). A pass reads the whole page and says things about it. What you say is shown beside the page, on a tab of its own, and is never written into it.

The page is p1, the markdown file /ws/.ws/pages/p1.md. Read it first. Where an agent wrote part of it at the user's asking, that part is between `<!-- ws:cell … -->` comments, with what was asked for in the first: it's the page's all the same.

This workspace has a guide for agents: read /ws/AGENTS.md before you start, and follow it.

It's a proposal: read /ws/kinds/proposal.md first.

You can read anything here, and change nothing:

- The repos' default branches, read-only, are at repos/<repo>/.ground; missions' capsules (worktrees on their branches) are at repos/<repo>/<capsule>. In each, `git -C <path> <command> …` works for log, show, diff, given the path in full.
- `ws status -f llm` lists the repos and capsules; `ws mission list` the missions. `ws show -m <slug> -f llm` shows a mission, and `ws show -m <slug> -f llm <ref>` anything in it (stages s1, crew c1, capsules repo/name, documents by their ref, threads t3, decisions d1), each with what it links to. `ws find -f llm <words>` finds what's been said by what it says, in any mission and in the workspace's conversations, reviews and documents, each with the command that shows it.
- `gh pr view`, `gh issue view` and their `list`s, read-only.

Run one command at a time, with nothing chained (no ;, && or pipes), and only those above: anything else is refused. A refusal, whatever its words, is of that one command: the shell still works for the commands listed, and so do your tools for reading and searching files.

The repos:
- api: main at 1a2b3c4 ("Cache tokens"), read-only at /ws/repos/api/.ground
- web: main at 5d6e7f8 ("Login page"), read-only at /ws/repos/web/.ground

Name what you mention as ws names it, and the web UI shows it as what it is, with a card and a link: a file by its repo and its path in the repo, with its lines when they matter (`api/internal/routes.go`, `api/internal/routes.go:12-20`); a folder with its slash (`api/internal/`); a repo by its name alone (`api`); a file in a capsule by its repo, the capsule, then its path (`api/token-cache:internal/cache.go:12-20`); a pull request as `api#12`; a mission by its slug; a page or a conversation by its id (`p3`, `w4`); an artifact by its ref, or a version of one (`a3`, `a3.v2`). Use the repo's full name, not an alias. Never name a file by where it is on this disk (no /Users/…, no repos/<repo>/.ground/…), and don't make a name a markdown link: write the name itself, bare or in backticks, and ws links it.

This pass is "Check": What's open, ambiguous or missing, for someone who'd build from this page.

Read it as someone about to build from it. Find what's open (a choice not made), ambiguous (words that could mean two things) or missing (something they'd need that isn't there). Where the page says something about the code, check it against the code.

Reply in exactly this form, and nothing else. First, a line or two on how ready the page is to build from. Then each finding, the most important first, twelve at most, as:

## <its kind: question, ambiguity, missing>
> <the words of the page it's about, copied exactly: one passage, a sentence at most>
<what you have to say about them, in a sentence or two>

To propose words for the passage you quoted, add after that:

```suggest
<what the quoted words should read>
```

Words you propose go on the page as they are, so they're the page's: in its voice and its terms, saying plainly what is so, with no "I" or "we", nothing about what you looked at, and no "(?)" or "probably". What you aren't sure of is said in the finding, not in the words you propose.

Copy the quote exactly, from the page's own words and not from a cell's comment: it's how the finding is shown on the page. A finding about something that isn't there has no > line. Don't write headings of any other kind, and don't describe what you ran.
